1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,786
A CTO can ask that in the next twelve
months, after reading a headline.

2
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:12,442
There are two tempting answers.
"Nothing, it is years away."

3
00:00:12,482 --> 00:00:16,402
And: "Everything, we need a migration
project." Both are guesses.

4
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:21,261
The honest answer has three parts, and each
comes from a different kind of source.

5
00:00:21,301 --> 00:00:25,875
What is planned is written in a document
that ships with your own Git installation.

6
00:00:25,915 --> 00:00:30,323
When it happens has one official sentence,
one primary announcement and a set of

7
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:32,881
secondary reports that
you must label as such.

8
00:00:33,221 --> 00:00:37,958
And what breaks for your team is something
you can find out this afternoon, in a

9
00:00:37,998 --> 00:00:42,496
sandbox, by switching the future defaults
on. Hold on to the CTO's question.

10
00:00:42,536 --> 00:00:47,333
Before the end you'll hear it answered in
three paragraphs, each with its source.

11
00:00:49,173 --> 00:00:53,802
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:53,842 --> 00:00:59,466
This is Part 11, the last part of the
course. Its three videos look ahead.

13
00:00:59,506 --> 00:01:03,905
Features that need Git 2.56
are named as such and not run.

14
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:05,665
The lab has Git 2.55.0.

15
00:01:06,005 --> 00:01:09,043
The skill this part trains
isn't knowing the answers.

16
00:01:09,083 --> 00:01:13,922
They will be out of date. It's finding them
in primary sources within a few minutes:

17
00:01:13,962 --> 00:01:18,684
the BreakingChanges document, the release
notes, the manual page of your installed

18
00:01:18,724 --> 00:01:20,020
version, and a sandbox.

19
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:25,139
From video 107 you know the two new
formats: SHA-256 as an object format and

20
00:01:25,179 --> 00:01:30,338
reftable as a ref backend. The object format
is the hash function that gives every

21
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:35,601
object its ID. The ref backend is how names
such as branches and tags are stored on

22
00:01:35,641 --> 00:01:40,737
disk. Today you see what the next major
version plans to do with them, which keys

23
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:45,809
choose the planned behavior ahead of time,
and what a team has to know before it

24
00:01:45,849 --> 00:01:48,472
lets any of this near a shared repository.

25
00:01:48,812 --> 00:01:52,263
Every statement about dates in
this video carries its source.

26
00:01:52,303 --> 00:01:56,899
Listen for the labels: official, primary
through a mirror, secondary, unverified.

27
00:01:58,739 --> 00:02:00,318
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:00,658 --> 00:02:02,070
After this video you can:

29
00:02:02,410 --> 00:02:07,714
State what Git 3.0 plans to change, for
which repositories, and what is officially

30
00:02:07,754 --> 00:02:09,018
said about its date.

31
00:02:09,358 --> 00:02:13,694
Name the configuration keys that choose
the planned defaults ahead of time and say

32
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:15,028
where you would set them.

33
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:20,073
Create a repository with the planned formats
today and convert the ref format of an

34
00:02:20,113 --> 00:02:20,816
existing one.

35
00:02:21,156 --> 00:02:25,456
Say what safe.bareRepository=explicit
refuses and allows.

36
00:02:25,796 --> 00:02:31,478
Say what would have to be true before a
hosted team repository can use SHA-256.

37
00:02:33,318 --> 00:02:39,536
What is official. The Git project keeps a
document named BreakingChanges, shipped

38
00:02:39,576 --> 00:02:45,639
with every installation. It says that
breaking releases happen rarely: 1.6.0 in

39
00:02:45,679 --> 00:02:50,583
August 2008, 2.0 in May 2014. It
lists what Git 3.0 will change.

40
00:02:50,623 --> 00:02:56,764
And it states: "There is no planned release
date for this breaking version yet."

41
00:02:56,804 --> 00:03:02,635
That sentence is in the copy installed
with Git 2.55.0 and, according to the

42
00:03:02,675 --> 00:03:06,189
course's research report,
unchanged at 2.56.0.

43
00:03:06,529 --> 00:03:11,155
The same document makes two promises.
The last version before 3.0 will be a

44
00:03:11,195 --> 00:03:16,381
long-term-support release, with important
bug fixes for at least four release cycles

45
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:21,545
and security fixes for six. And every
breaking change is guarded by a build switch,

46
00:03:21,585 --> 00:03:26,584
WITH_BREAKING_CHANGES, so that the future
behavior can be tested before it ships.

47
00:03:26,924 --> 00:03:32,177
What the maintainer announced. Git 2.56.0
was released on 28 September 2026.

48
00:03:32,217 --> 00:03:37,819
On the same day the maintainer wrote that
the next version will be numbered 2.98,

49
00:03:37,859 --> 00:03:43,251
"scheduled near the end of this year",
to be followed by 2.99 "to solidify the

50
00:03:43,291 --> 00:03:48,683
codebase in preparation for Git 3.0".
The textbook read that message through a

51
00:03:48,723 --> 00:03:52,723
mailing-list mirror. The jump
in the number is the signal.

52
00:03:53,063 --> 00:03:59,058
What only secondary sources say. Calendar
months, and the relation between 2.99 and

53
00:03:59,098 --> 00:04:01,821
3.0, are not in any official document.

54
00:04:02,161 --> 00:04:06,398
The first row is official, the second is
primary, read through a mirror, and the

55
00:04:06,438 --> 00:04:07,735
last three are secondary.

56
00:04:08,075 --> 00:04:12,653
Every date in the last three rows of the
table. Treat them as a plan reported from

57
00:04:12,693 --> 00:04:17,270
conference talks, and re-check BreakingChanges
and the release announcement before

58
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:19,354
you put a date into a migration plan.

59
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:25,778
The planned defaults. All of the following
is from BreakingChanges as installed with

60
00:04:25,818 --> 00:04:26,580
Git 2.55.0.

61
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:32,076
Read the table as a plan, row by row. New
repositories would use SHA-256 as their

62
00:04:32,116 --> 00:04:37,336
hash function, when libraries, applications
and forges are ready, and SHA-1 is not

63
00:04:37,376 --> 00:04:42,661
being deprecated. Their refs would be stored
with reftable, which JGit, libgit2 and

64
00:04:42,701 --> 00:04:46,830
Gitoxide need to support. The
initial branch would be named main.

65
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:51,128
A bare repository found by walking
up directories would be refused.

66
00:04:51,168 --> 00:04:56,516
And Rust would be mandatory in the build,
which may be deferred if distributions are

67
00:04:56,556 --> 00:04:57,094
hit hard.

68
00:04:57,434 --> 00:05:02,478
The removals. Grafts, replaced by
git replace. git pack-redundant.

69
00:05:02,518 --> 00:05:07,947
The directories .git/branches/ and
.git/remotes/ as sources of remotes.

70
00:05:07,987 --> 00:05:11,876
git name-rev --stdin,
replaced by --annotate-stdin.

71
00:05:11,916 --> 00:05:16,421
git whatchanged. And the values
core.commentString=auto and

72
00:05:16,461 --> 00:05:22,738
core.preferSymlinkRefs=true. One section
of the document records a decision not to

73
00:05:22,778 --> 00:05:28,285
remove something: git checkout stays
next to git switch and git restore.

74
00:05:28,625 --> 00:05:32,368
Quick quiz. Suppose Git 3.0
is released and you upgrade.

75
00:05:32,408 --> 00:05:37,908
What happens to the repositories you already
have? A, they're converted to the new

76
00:05:37,948 --> 00:05:41,083
formats. B, nothing
converts them. Your answer?

77
00:05:45,923 --> 00:05:49,783
Three points keep the scale in
proportion. The answer is B.

78
00:05:49,823 --> 00:05:53,484
The changed defaults apply
when a repository is created.

79
00:05:53,524 --> 00:05:58,508
Nothing converts an existing repository.
A proposal to accept only lowercase

80
00:05:58,548 --> 00:06:03,994
hexadecimal object IDs was still waiting
for review on 28 September 2026 and is not

81
00:06:04,034 --> 00:06:07,365
a decision. And hosting
decides more than Git does.

82
00:06:07,705 --> 00:06:12,919
Opting in, in one sentence. Four configuration
keys select, for the repositories you

83
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:17,861
create, the behavior that Git 3.0 will
make the default, and the same keys keep

84
00:06:17,901 --> 00:06:19,613
today's behavior afterwards.

85
00:06:19,953 --> 00:06:25,159
safe.bareRepository. The fourth key is
about which repositories Git agrees to use.

86
00:06:25,199 --> 00:06:30,149
A bare repository is one with no working
tree, such as the one a server holds.

87
00:06:30,189 --> 00:06:34,180
With explicit, a bare repository
is used only when you name it.

88
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:39,362
Fetching from it, pushing to it and cloning
it still work, because those commands

89
00:06:39,402 --> 00:06:44,480
name the repository, and a .git directory
inside a working tree is not "bare" in

90
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:45,184
this sense.

91
00:06:45,524 --> 00:06:49,165
The reason for the change is
a case you met in video 163.

92
00:06:49,205 --> 00:06:54,525
A clone never brings a .git/config. It can
bring a tracked directory that is shaped

93
00:06:54,565 --> 00:06:58,787
like a bare repository, with a
HEAD, a config, objects/ and refs/.

94
00:06:58,827 --> 00:07:04,212
Step into that directory, and with the
current default Git takes it for a repository

95
00:07:04,252 --> 00:07:08,991
and reads the configuration file that
arrived as ordinary tracked content.

96
00:07:09,331 --> 00:07:14,266
BreakingChanges describes the attack in
which such a file names a command, through

97
00:07:14,306 --> 00:07:18,816
core.fsmonitor for example, that runs
when a shell prompt calls git status.

98
00:07:19,156 --> 00:07:25,610
SHA-256 and reftable, locally. Two practical
facts complete what video 107 taught.

99
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:29,327
History can cross the format
boundary only as a stream.

100
00:07:29,367 --> 00:07:34,546
git fast-export writes history as text and
git fast-import rebuilds it in the format

101
00:07:34,586 --> 00:07:38,026
of the receiving repository.
Every object gets a new ID.

102
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:43,183
Signatures do not survive, and commit IDs
quoted in messages, issue trackers and CI

103
00:07:43,223 --> 00:07:46,477
configuration point at nothing
in the new repository.

104
00:07:46,517 --> 00:07:50,950
A conversion is a migration project
with a cut-over date, not a setting.

105
00:07:51,290 --> 00:07:56,944
The ref format can change in place and back.
git refs migrate --ref-format=reftable

106
00:07:56,984 --> 00:08:02,570
converts an existing repository, with the
limits the manual lists: not with linked

107
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:06,548
worktrees, and no concurrent
writers during the migration.

108
00:08:06,588 --> 00:08:11,831
Code that reads .git/refs/heads/<branch>
breaks at that moment, and code that

109
00:08:11,871 --> 00:08:15,329
expects forty hexadecimal
digits breaks on SHA-256.

110
00:08:15,369 --> 00:08:20,543
The repair is the habit this course has
used since Part 4: ask Git, with git

111
00:08:20,583 --> 00:08:25,688
rev-parse, git for-each-ref or git refs
list, and never read the directory.

112
00:08:26,028 --> 00:08:31,691
GitHub, not Git. GitHub had no publicly
available support for SHA-256 repositories

113
00:08:31,731 --> 00:08:37,115
on 1 October 2026. A private preview is
reported by GitLab's blog, a community

114
00:08:37,155 --> 00:08:42,470
comment and a conference speaker, and
no GitHub blog post, changelog entry or

115
00:08:42,510 --> 00:08:47,547
documentation page announces it, so
the course's research report marks it

116
00:08:47,587 --> 00:08:53,389
unverified. Whether a host stores refs with
reftable on its servers does not concern

117
00:08:53,429 --> 00:08:55,406
you: the ref format is local.

118
00:08:55,746 --> 00:09:01,336
So, for objective five: before a hosted
team repository can use SHA-256, the host

119
00:09:01,376 --> 00:09:06,967
has to accept it, and so do the libraries
and applications around the repository:

120
00:09:07,007 --> 00:09:12,736
the condition in the first row of the table.
Until then a repository that must live

121
00:09:12,776 --> 00:09:14,544
on github.com stays SHA-1.

122
00:09:14,884 --> 00:09:18,517
In production. The textbook's
advice has three lines.

123
00:09:18,557 --> 00:09:23,160
Set init.defaultBranch=main everywhere
now. It is what 3.0 will do.

124
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:28,773
Set safe.bareRepository=explicit on developer
machines now, and check the scripts

125
00:09:28,813 --> 00:09:34,040
that cd into bare repositories, backup
jobs, mirror scripts, self-hosted Git

126
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:39,446
servers, before 3.0 does it for you.
Leave the object and ref formats at their

127
00:09:39,486 --> 00:09:44,782
defaults in shared configuration until
your hosting and your tools are ready.

128
00:09:46,622 --> 00:09:51,330
A picture helps. Think of a country that
announces it will change the side of the

129
00:09:51,370 --> 00:09:55,257
road on which new roads are built.
Existing roads stay as they are.

130
00:09:55,297 --> 00:10:00,180
No date is fixed. The transport ministry
publishes the plan in a document anyone can

131
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:04,166
read, and offers a test track where
you can drive the new way today.

132
00:10:04,506 --> 00:10:08,117
The questions a sensible
haulage company asks are yours.

133
00:10:08,157 --> 00:10:12,812
Which of our vehicles assume the old
side? That is the script that reads

134
00:10:12,852 --> 00:10:16,790
.git/refs/heads/main by hand.
Can we drive on the test track?

135
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:22,267
That is the sandbox with the four keys set.
And what do the newspapers say about the

136
00:10:22,307 --> 00:10:27,157
date, as opposed to the ministry? That
is the table with its status column.

137
00:10:27,497 --> 00:10:32,294
Where the picture breaks: roads are shared,
and two of these four changes are not.

138
00:10:32,334 --> 00:10:36,364
The ref format is local to each clone
and the protocol never sees it.

139
00:10:36,404 --> 00:10:40,847
The object format is the opposite: it
is shared by everything that exchanges

140
00:10:40,887 --> 00:10:43,088
objects, which is why hosting decides.

141
00:10:44,928 --> 00:10:50,182
Say each zone's kind of source as you
mark it. Released, which you can install.

142
00:10:50,222 --> 00:10:54,605
Announced, by the maintainer.
Reported only, by secondary sources.

143
00:10:54,645 --> 00:11:00,168
The words "LTS" under 2.99 and "with 2.99"
under 3.0 are in the third zone although

144
00:11:00,208 --> 00:11:05,596
the version numbers above them are in the
second. That is exactly the distinction

145
00:11:05,636 --> 00:11:07,740
the interview question asks for.

146
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:12,060
What this Git was built with. 🟢 SAFE.

147
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:17,576
Three lines to remember: Rust disabled,
default ref format files, default hash sha1.

148
00:11:17,616 --> 00:11:22,606
The same command on a later Git is how you
check what your installation was built

149
00:11:22,646 --> 00:11:22,917
with.

150
00:11:23,257 --> 00:11:28,540
Try it now, in any repository of your own.
Thirty seconds, and it's read-only: git

151
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:32,824
rev-parse --show-object-format.
Predict first: what will it print?

152
00:11:37,664 --> 00:11:42,226
If it printed sha1, that's today's
default, the one the build options named.

153
00:11:42,266 --> 00:11:47,313
And notice how you found out: you asked Git,
and you didn't read the dot git folder.

154
00:11:47,653 --> 00:11:52,012
An unconfigured git init today. The
lab configuration normally sets

155
00:11:52,052 --> 00:11:56,870
init.defaultBranch, so the demo removes
it first. Predict the branch name.

156
00:11:56,910 --> 00:11:57,920
Say it out loud.

157
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:08,344
master, with a hint, and the hint names
Git 3.0. The formats: sha1 and files.

158
00:12:08,684 --> 00:12:14,436
Opt in. 🟡 CAUTION: git config set --global
changes the global configuration file.

159
00:12:14,476 --> 00:12:17,367
Nothing changes in existing repositories.

160
00:12:17,707 --> 00:12:23,275
main, sha256, reftable. Look at the
configuration file of the new repository:

161
00:12:23,315 --> 00:12:27,353
repositoryformatversion = 1
and an [extensions] section.

162
00:12:27,393 --> 00:12:32,087
That makes older Git versions
refuse it instead of misreading it.

163
00:12:32,427 --> 00:12:37,742
New repositories only. Predict: the old
repository, and a clone of it made now.

164
00:12:37,782 --> 00:12:39,844
Which formats? Say it out loud.

165
00:12:44,684 --> 00:12:49,338
The existing repository did not change.
Its clone took the object format from the

166
00:12:49,378 --> 00:12:54,089
source, as it must, and the ref format from
your setting, because ref storage is a

167
00:12:54,129 --> 00:12:58,783
local matter that the protocol never sees.
One line of output, and both halves of

168
00:12:58,823 --> 00:13:00,348
the mental model are in it.

169
00:13:00,688 --> 00:13:06,447
Opt out. The same keys keep the old behavior
after 3.0, for the tools that need it.

170
00:13:06,787 --> 00:13:08,520
The removals announce themselves.

171
00:13:08,860 --> 00:13:14,883
On Git 2.55, git whatchanged refuses to
run without a flag that says you still use

172
00:13:14,923 --> 00:13:21,021
it, exits with 128, and prints its own
replacement: git log with --raw --no-merges.

173
00:13:21,061 --> 00:13:26,714
A script that calls it has already failed.
That's the kind of thing an opt-in

174
00:13:26,754 --> 00:13:27,897
afternoon finds.

175
00:13:28,237 --> 00:13:30,024
explicit in a bare repository.

176
00:13:30,364 --> 00:13:34,896
The same command, before and after the
setting. After it: "cannot use bare

177
00:13:34,936 --> 00:13:40,086
repository", with the reason in parentheses.
Naming the repository with --git-dir is

178
00:13:40,126 --> 00:13:41,630
what "explicit" asks for.

179
00:13:41,970 --> 00:13:45,022
What still works: the commands
that name the repository.

180
00:13:45,362 --> 00:13:50,172
The embedded bare repository. The setting
is back at its default for this step.

181
00:13:50,212 --> 00:13:53,671
You clone a project and step
into one of its directories.

182
00:13:53,711 --> 00:13:56,863
Predict what git rev-parse
--git-dir will say there.

183
00:13:56,903 --> 00:13:57,845
Say it out loud.

184
00:14:02,685 --> 00:14:07,862
Git found a repository in a tracked directory
and read a config file that arrived as

185
00:14:07,902 --> 00:14:11,899
ordinary content of the clone. The
demo stops at reading a value.

186
00:14:11,939 --> 00:14:13,327
The value is a command.

187
00:14:13,667 --> 00:14:18,497
With explicit, Git refuses the directory,
the configuration value is not read, and

188
00:14:18,537 --> 00:14:21,406
the real repository one
level up works as before.

189
00:14:21,746 --> 00:14:25,267
Today: a SHA-1 repository
and a script from 2019.

190
00:14:28,607 --> 00:14:32,716
git repo info --all prints the
layout and the two formats.

191
00:14:32,756 --> 00:14:38,653
Then read the release script: it reads
.git/refs/heads/main with cat and checks for

192
00:14:38,693 --> 00:14:44,589
exactly forty hexadecimal digits. It works
today. Predict two ways in which it will

193
00:14:44,629 --> 00:14:46,878
stop working. Say them out loud.

194
00:14:51,718 --> 00:14:56,123
Keep your two predictions. Lab
42.1 is where you check them.

195
00:14:56,463 --> 00:15:02,034
The future: both formats at creation. 🟢
SAFE: git init creates a new repository.

196
00:15:05,374 --> 00:15:10,690
A reftable directory in .git. HEAD is a
stub that names refs/heads/.invalid: with

197
00:15:10,730 --> 00:15:16,112
reftable, the file exists for tools that
look for it, and the real value is in the

198
00:15:16,152 --> 00:15:19,484
table. And the commit ID
has sixty-four characters.

199
00:15:19,524 --> 00:15:22,526
git refs list reads refs
whatever the backend.

200
00:15:22,866 --> 00:15:28,046
Converting the object format. Predict what
a fetch between the two repositories will

201
00:15:28,086 --> 00:15:29,351
say. Say it out loud.

202
00:15:34,191 --> 00:15:37,937
"Mismatched algorithms": the two
formats cannot exchange objects.

203
00:15:38,277 --> 00:15:44,172
The stream carries the history across. The
subjects match and no ID does: 53a79b6 on

204
00:15:44,212 --> 00:15:47,846
one side, 587a0ea on the
other, for the same commit.

205
00:15:48,186 --> 00:15:53,283
Migrating the ref format in place. 🟡 CAUTION:
git refs migrate rewrites how refs are

206
00:15:53,323 --> 00:15:58,357
stored in this repository. Preview: git
for-each-ref before, to compare afterwards.

207
00:15:58,397 --> 00:16:03,127
It can be migrated back. Not with linked
worktrees, and no concurrent writers.

208
00:16:03,467 --> 00:16:07,174
The same three refs with the
same IDs before and after.

209
00:16:07,214 --> 00:16:11,738
Nothing a Git command can see has
changed. What changed is what cat

210
00:16:11,778 --> 00:16:16,370
.git/refs/heads/main can see, and
that's the lab's failure scenario.

211
00:16:18,210 --> 00:16:19,858
Five mistakes to watch for.

212
00:16:20,198 --> 00:16:25,250
Quoting a release month for Git 3.0 as a
fact. Root cause: the official document

213
00:16:25,290 --> 00:16:30,533
says there is no planned date; the months
come from secondary reports of conference

214
00:16:30,573 --> 00:16:30,915
talks.

215
00:16:31,255 --> 00:16:36,651
Expecting 3.0 to convert existing repositories.
Root cause: the planned changes are

216
00:16:36,691 --> 00:16:42,152
defaults applied when a repository is created,
plus removals of deprecated features.

217
00:16:42,492 --> 00:16:46,847
Setting SHA-256 as the default
in shared configuration now.

218
00:16:46,887 --> 00:16:53,106
Root cause: the object format is shared by
everything that exchanges objects, and on

219
00:16:53,146 --> 00:16:58,097
1 October 2026 github.com had no
publicly available support for it.

220
00:16:58,437 --> 00:17:03,515
Reading refs from files in scripts. Root
cause: with reftable there is no file per

221
00:17:03,555 --> 00:17:08,633
branch, so only commands such as git
rev-parse and git for-each-ref give the value

222
00:17:08,673 --> 00:17:09,694
on every backend.

223
00:17:10,034 --> 00:17:15,097
Treating a format conversion as a setting.
Root cause: every object gets a new ID,

224
00:17:15,137 --> 00:17:20,325
signatures do not survive, and every recorded
commit ID points at nothing in the new

225
00:17:20,365 --> 00:17:21,009
repository.

226
00:17:22,849 --> 00:17:27,994
Now, out of the lab. A platform engineer
is asked the hook's question by her CTO on

227
00:17:28,034 --> 00:17:32,616
a Monday. She answers on Tuesday, with
three paragraphs and their sources.

228
00:17:32,956 --> 00:17:38,333
What is planned: new defaults for new
repositories and a list of removals, from the

229
00:17:38,373 --> 00:17:41,793
BreakingChanges document
installed on her own laptop.

230
00:17:41,833 --> 00:17:46,753
When: no official date. The maintainer
has announced versions 2.98 and 2.99.

231
00:17:46,793 --> 00:17:52,040
The months in circulation are from two
secondary sources, and she labels them so.

232
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:55,434
What breaks for us: she
spent the afternoon on that.

233
00:17:55,774 --> 00:18:00,800
In a sandbox with the four keys set, she
ran the team's release tooling and its

234
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:05,866
backup job. Two things failed. A release
script read a branch file with cat and

235
00:18:05,906 --> 00:18:11,252
checked for forty characters. And the nightly
mirror job changed directory into bare

236
00:18:11,292 --> 00:18:16,318
repositories and ran git log there, which
safe.bareRepository=explicit refuses.

237
00:18:16,658 --> 00:18:20,048
Her recommendation follows
the textbook's three lines.

238
00:18:20,088 --> 00:18:25,383
init.defaultBranch=main everywhere, today.
safe.bareRepository=explicit on developer

239
00:18:25,423 --> 00:18:29,893
machines today, after the mirror job
is changed to name its repository.

240
00:18:29,933 --> 00:18:34,911
Object and ref formats left at their
defaults in shared configuration, with one

241
00:18:34,951 --> 00:18:40,119
sentence on why: the host. The two scripts
are fixed that week, and they are fixed

242
00:18:40,159 --> 00:18:44,883
in the durable way: they ask Git. And
that's the question from the opening,

243
00:18:44,923 --> 00:18:47,996
answered: three paragraphs,
each with its source.

244
00:18:49,836 --> 00:18:56,618
Your turn. Do Lab 42.1, "SHA-256
and reftable repositories", in

245
00:18:56,658 --> 00:18:59,542
lab-manual/m42-frontier.md.

246
00:18:59,882 --> 00:19:04,635
Before you create the repository with the
planned formats, predict what .git will

247
00:19:04,675 --> 00:19:09,014
contain that a repository of today does
not, and what .git/HEAD will hold.

248
00:19:09,054 --> 00:19:13,689
Before the conversion, predict which of
these survive it: subjects, commit IDs,

249
00:19:13,729 --> 00:19:18,423
tags, signatures. Before the lab's failure
scenario, read the release script and

250
00:19:18,463 --> 00:19:23,216
write down the two lines that will break
and in which repository each will break.

251
00:19:23,556 --> 00:19:28,377
The challenge: opt in to the planned
defaults in a sandbox configuration and run

252
00:19:28,417 --> 00:19:32,023
your own team's Git scripts
there. List what breaks and why.

253
00:19:32,063 --> 00:19:35,365
Use a sandbox configuration,
not your real global file.

254
00:19:37,205 --> 00:19:39,258
Read the question, then answer out loud.

255
00:19:44,098 --> 00:19:47,315
The question has four parts and
rewards separation of sources.

256
00:19:47,355 --> 00:19:51,518
A strong answer lists the changes in two
groups, defaults and removals, and says

257
00:19:51,558 --> 00:19:54,197
precisely to which repositories
the defaults apply.

258
00:19:54,237 --> 00:19:58,453
It gives the official statement about the
date as what it is, one sentence in one

259
00:19:58,493 --> 00:20:00,187
document, and names the document.

260
00:20:00,527 --> 00:20:05,107
It then separates what the maintainer
announced from what only secondary sources

261
00:20:05,147 --> 00:20:09,786
report, and labels the last group as
unverified. It ends on what a team should do

262
00:20:09,826 --> 00:20:14,465
with that uncertainty, which is not to
wait: name the two settings worth adopting

263
00:20:14,505 --> 00:20:18,046
now and the two worth leaving
alone, with the reason for each.

264
00:20:19,886 --> 00:20:20,813
Let's land this.

265
00:20:21,153 --> 00:20:27,573
Git 3.0 plans new defaults for newly created
repositories, SHA-256, reftable, main,

266
00:20:27,613 --> 00:20:33,956
safe.bareRepository=explicit, mandatory
Rust in the build, and a list of removals;

267
00:20:33,996 --> 00:20:37,148
existing repositories keep their formats.

268
00:20:37,488 --> 00:20:42,997
Officially there is no planned release
date; the maintainer has announced 2.98 and

269
00:20:43,037 --> 00:20:46,381
2.99; calendar months come
from secondary sources.

270
00:20:46,721 --> 00:20:52,161
Four keys choose the planned behavior
today and keep the old behavior later:

271
00:20:52,201 --> 00:20:57,280
init.defaultObjectFormat,
init.defaultRefFormat, init.defaultBranch and

272
00:20:57,320 --> 00:20:58,723
safe.bareRepository.

273
00:20:59,063 --> 00:21:03,860
The ref format is local and can be migrated
in place; the object format is shared,

274
00:21:03,900 --> 00:21:07,222
changes every ID when converted,
and depends on the host.

275
00:21:07,562 --> 00:21:11,580
Scripts ask Git for refs and IDs;
they never read .git by hand.

276
00:21:13,420 --> 00:21:21,666
Read sections 14D.1 to 14D.5. Then find
the BreakingChanges document of your own

277
00:21:21,706 --> 00:21:30,055
installation, in the directory that git
--html-path prints, and find the sentence

278
00:21:30,095 --> 00:21:36,062
about the release date. Video
201 shows the exact command.

279
00:21:36,102 --> 00:21:41,551
Check whether it still reads
as quoted in this video.

280
00:21:41,591 --> 00:21:49,111
If it doesn't, you've learned the most
important thing this part teaches.

281
00:21:49,451 --> 00:21:54,161
Today you separated a plan from a rumour,
and you tried the planned defaults in a

282
00:21:54,201 --> 00:21:59,087
sandbox, where nothing of yours could break.
Next time: git history, git replay, git

283
00:21:59,127 --> 00:22:03,778
last-modified and git repo. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

284
00:22:03,818 --> 00:22:05,186
See you in the next one.
